Chapter: ProbeClosureLetform

 Same chapter as probe-closure-contagion -- make-adder plus a defined,
 never-called relay-once -- with ONE thing changed: the driver binds the
 intermediate closure to a local and applies both remaining arguments to
 it, instead of writing one flattened chain from the global name.

 The in-tree fixture codex/plugs/test-input/partial.codex:9-10 uses
 exactly this shape, and it is the only shape the tree exercises.

 `letform 47` means the chain form and the let form compile differently
 and the defect is in the chain form's path, not in make-adder. A fault
 means the two forms share it and the local binding is irrelevant.

Section: Shapes

  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (x) (y) (z) = x + y + z

  make-adder : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  make-adder (n) = add3 n

 Defined, never called. Present so this chapter matches
 probe-closure-contagion, which faulted.

  relay-once : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  relay-once (n) = make-adder n

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let f = make-adder 5
    in print-line-uni ("letform " & show ((f 20) 22))
  end
